::GEO_U05_L04_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L04_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.4
Question:
From exterior point 1, a tangent has length t and a secant has external segment 4 and whole secant 9. What is t?
{
~A. 13#Adding the secant parts does not use the tangent-secant theorem.
=B. 6#Use tangent-secant: t? = external segment times whole secant = 4 times 9, so t = 6.
~C. 5#Subtracting does not give the tangent length.
~D. 36#This is t?, not t.
}
::GEO_U05_L04_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L04_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.4
Question:
Two chords intersect inside a circle. One chord has segments 3 and 8; the other has segments 4 and x. What is x?
{
~A. 15#Adding segment lengths does not use the chord product relationship.
~B. 24#This is the product on one chord, not the missing segment.
~C. Cannot be determined#The missing segment can be determined because the intersecting chord product relationship applies.
=D. 6#Use intersecting chords: 3 times 8 = 4 times x, so x = 6.
}
::GEO_U05_L04_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L04_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.4
Question:
Two secants are drawn from the same exterior point. The first has external segment 3 and whole length 12. The second has external segment 4 and whole length x. What is x?
{
=A. 9#Use secant-secant: 3 times 12 = 4 times x, so x = 9.
~B. 11#This combines lengths additively instead of using products.
~C. 36#This is the product for the first secant, not x.
~D. 5#This stays near the external segment but does not use the secant-secant product relationship.
}
::GEO_U05_L04_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L04_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.4
Question:
A tangent from exterior point 4 touches a circle. The radius to the tangent point is 5 and the distance from the center to the exterior point is 13. What is the tangent length?
{
~A. 8#Subtracting the lengths ignores the right-triangle relationship.
~B. 18#Adding the lengths does not find the tangent leg.
=C. 12#The radius to a tangent is perpendicular, so use the Pythagorean Theorem: tangent length is 12.
~D. 65#Multiplying does not find the missing leg.
}
::GEO_U05_L04_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L04_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.4
Question:
Which equation correctly represents a tangent-secant relationship?
{
~A. t \= (external segment)(inside segment)#This omits the square on the tangent and uses the wrong secant length.
=B. t? \= (external segment)(whole secant)#The tangent-secant theorem squares the tangent length and equals external times whole secant.
~C. t? \= (inside segment)(whole secant)#The theorem uses the external segment, not just the inside part.
~D. t + external \= whole secant#This is an additive relationship, not the tangent-secant theorem.
}
